Picture \par
\par
Who are the important characters in John Webster\rquote s The Duchess of Malfi? It is a simple question but the play\rquote s slightly odd structure makes the answer more complicated than it might at first appear. The Duchess, as the title character, is clearly the centre around which the play is oriented, but she dies in Act IV, leaving the finale to others. Bosola is central to Act V, and there is evidence to suggest that Webster thought hard about his role in the play. For example, Bosola has a roughly equal number of lines to the Duchess (more than any other character) and he appears at the head of the male cast list in the play\rquote s original publication, just as the Duchess appears at the head of the female cast list. \par
\par
Several pieces of circumstantial evidence also support Bosola\rquote s claim to importance. In the original production at the Globe sometime in 1613 or 1614, he was played by John Lowin, one of the leading actors in the King\rquote s Men, who had previously played Shakespeare\rquote s Falstaff. The King\rquote s Men had performed Anthony and Cleopatra the year before they first staged The Duchess of Malfi and it might be the case that Webster was inspired to create a pair of protagonists \f1\emdash  the Duchess and Bosola \emdash  in a structure comparable to Shakespeare\rquote s. \par
\par
The source for Bosola \par
\par
Like most early modern playwrights, Webster did not invent the story of his play but borrowed it from pre-existing sources. For this play, he drew on William Painter\rquote s tale, Palace of Pleasure (1567). What is significant here is that, in modifying his source, Webster puts greater emphasis on Bosola. Michael Neill has written that in Painter\rquote s version, Bozola (sic) is \lquote barely more than a name, a thuggish soldier who only appears at the very end of the tale when the Duchess\rquote s brothers hire him to murder Antonio\rquote  (Neill, p. xix). The details that Webster adds include the brothers\rquote  hiring of Bosola as their informer in the Duchess\rquote s household, Bosola\rquote s discovery of the horoscope relating to her child, and his devising of the torments for the Duchess before her death. Perhaps most significantly, Webster also alters Bosola\rquote s fate \emdash  in Painter, he escapes after carrying out the contract killing, while in Webster he dies, so conforming to the conventions of the tragic hero. \par
\par
Bosola\rquote s backstory \par
\par
What we learn of Bosola\rquote s life before the action of the play makes him quite a complex mix. He expresses a paradox from the opening, where Delio has him as a \lquote notorious murderer\rquote  and Antonio, in the very next speech, notes that \lquote he\rquote s very valiant\rquote  (I. i. 68\endash 73). Though he is a renowned murderer and has spent time as a galley slave, we also hear much later from Delio of Bosola\rquote s original ambitions: \par
\par
I knew him in Padua \emdash a fantastical scholar, like such who study to know how many knots was in Hercules\rquote  club, of what colour Achilles\rquote  beard was, or whether Hector were not troubled with the toothache. He hath studied himself half blear-eyed to know the true symmetry of Caesar\rquote s nose by a shoeing-horn; and this he did to gain the name of a speculative man. \par
\par
(III. iii. 41\endash 47) \par
\par
At the time of the play, Padua was the home of one of Europe\rquote s most celebrated universities; Hercules, Achilles and Hector are characters in classical myth and literature (i.e. a vital part of early modern education); and a \lquote speculative man\rquote  was one interested in theory and reasoning. In short, Bosola is a learned man, a philosopher in the mould of Hamlet, except he is also a man of action. \par
\par
This backstory links Bosola to important historical contexts for the play. Tudor and Stuart England had seen a rapid expansion of education with a larger number of university graduates than ever before. However, the offices of church and state, where most graduates had previously found work and station, did not expand at the same rate. So, there was a surplus of highly educated but underemployed young men who were seen as potential instigators of rebellion. One social historian calls them \lquote alienated intellectuals\rquote  (Curtis, p. 25). The authorities at the time recognised the danger: Lord Chancellor Ellesmere, in 1611, warned that \lquote learning without living doth breed traitors as common experience too well showeth\rquote  (cited in Curtis, p. 28). Bosola, a scholar now reduced to a mercenary, certainly fits this category and his first conversation with the Cardinal shows just how disenfranchised he is: \lquote I have done you / Better service than to be slighted thus. / Miserable age, where only the reward / of doing well is the doing of it\rquote  (I. i. 29\endash 32). His \lquote intelligence\rquote  suits him to become an \lquote intelligencer\rquote , that is, a spy. \par
\par
Significant contexts for Bosola \par
\par
Bosola\rquote s characteristics are embedded in early modern culture and as such it proves fruitful to study him in relation to four \lquote M\rquote s: Machiavelli, mercenaries, melancholy and the malcontent. \par
\par
Machiavelli \par
\par
Niccolo Machiavelli was the Italian author of The Prince (1532), an early work of political philosophy that was incredibly widely disseminated. The book offers advice for rulers on how to hold and exercise power. It emphasises pragmatism over idealism and, if acted upon, its recommendations would amount to tyranny. It shocked contemporaries with its disregard for morality. In The Duchess of Malfi, Bosola is the character most adept in deploying Machiavelli\rquote s principles. All Webster\rquote s characters (including the Duchess) play the game \emdash  dissemble, spy, keep secrets, manipulate \emdash  but Bosola plays it best. He is the most opaque in motivation; he is most suited to the adjective \lquote Machiavellian\rquote . \par
\par
Mercenaries \par
\par
But, Bosola is not a prince, he is a servant. He refers to himself as Ferdinand\rquote s \lquote creature\rquote  (I. i. 278). We are told that he has previously been a soldier. Mercenaries were (or are) a class of soldier who fight for financial gain rather than for political interests or loyalty to a country or cause. They were often involved in the conflicts of early modern Europe, before the existence of professional national armies. Bosola, perhaps through necessity, thinks like a mercenary: for him, need often overrides loyalty. He resists but then accepts Ferdinand\rquote s pay: \lquote oh that to avoid ingratitude / For the good deed you have done me, I must do / All the ill man can invent\rquote  (I. i. 264\endash 66). However, given the conflicting models of motivation present in his character, the play might be said to dramatise a transition in Bosola. \par
\par
Picture \par
\par
Melancholy \par
\par
Melancholy has a strong presence in The Duchess of Malfi. While the term is still current, in the early modern period it carried more particular meanings than it does now. At that time, anatomists and physicians viewed characteristics and moods to be the result of imbalances of the \lquote humours\rquote  in the body. In this system of thought, melancholy was a mental disease thought to be the result of an excess of black bile. Timothy Bright\rquote s Treatise on Melancholy (1586) and Robert Burton\rquote s The Anatomy of Melancholy (1621) are large-scale works demonstrating the currency of these ideas in Webster\rquote s time. \par
\par
Melancholy is linked to a number of characters (for example, Antonio calls the Cardinal \lquote a melancholy churchman\rquote ) but is most strongly associated with Bosola throughout the play. Antonio says of him that \lquote This foul melancholy / Will poison all his goodness\rquote  (I. i. 74\endash 75). But, Bosola is a slippery character who doesn\rquote t always disclose his motivations to other characters or to the audience. Is Bosola merely playing the role of the melancholic (as Antonio suspects in II. i) and if so, why? It might be that this guise provides some cover for Bosola\rquote s real intentions, which lie elsewhere. \par
\par
The malcontent \par
\par
The malcontent is a common character type in early modern drama. He is dissatisfied with social structures and/or other characters. Well-known examples include Iago in Othello, Jacques in As You Like It and Hamlet. In 1604, Webster wrote some additional passages for a new staging of John Marston\rquote s play The Malcontent, demonstrating that he was well aware of the tradition. Brian Gibbons writes about this figure in relation to The Duchess of Malfi: \par
\par
In Jacobean revenge tragedies generally the figure of the malcontent is not just a hired contract-killer, he is given much more prominence and stronger motivation, interwoven in the drama\rquote s whole configuration. [\'85] What is striking in Webster is that Bosola seems at the beginning a mere assassin; he is only gradually drawn towards the inner world of the heroine; his sense of righteousness and justice revived by contact with her, but even so he still acts as a tool of villainy and persecution. \par
\par
(Gibbons, p. xvii) \par
\par
The central questions that this prompts are: \par
\par
\f2\u9632?\f0  Does Bosola change (in a play in which most other characters do not)? \par
\par
\f2\u9632?\f0  If so, can we identify at what point he changes and how and why he changes? \par
\par
Bosola\rquote s transition \par
\par
It might seem that extreme cynicism is Bosola\rquote s governing character trait. The play overflows with his speeches amply demonstrating this. Here are a few lines: \par
\par
\lquote Thus the devil \par
Candies all sins o\rquote er: and what heaven terms vile, \par
That names he complemental.\rquote  \par
\par
(I. i. 266\f1\endash 68) \par
\par
\lquote Though we are eaten up of lice, and worms, \par
And though continually we bear about us \par
A rotten and dead body, we delight \par
To hide it in rich tissue.\rquote  \par
\par
(II. i. 253\endash 56) \par
\par
\lquote What\rquote s this flesh? A little cruded milk, fantastical puff paste: our bodies are weaker than those paper prisons boys use to keep flies in \emdash  more contemptible, since ours is to preserve earth worms.\rquote  \par
\par
(IV. ii. 116\endash 19) \par
\par
But, when Bosola finally learns from the Duchess that she married Antonio for love rather than position, politics or any ulterior motive, he seems to experience it as an epiphany: \par
\par
\lquote Do I not dream? Can this ambitious age \par
Have so much goodness in\rquote t as to prefer \par
A man merely for worth, without these shadows \par
Of wealth and painted honours? Possible? \par
[\'85] \par
\par
                                Fortunate lady, \par
For you have made your private nuptial bed \par
The humble and fair seminary of peace, \par
No question but many an unbeneficed scholar \par
Shall pray for you, for this deed, and rejoice \par
That some preferment in this world can yet \par
Arise from merit.\rquote  \par
\par
(III. i. 273\endash 83) \par
\par
Perhaps he has himself in mind as an \lquote unbeneficed scholar\rquote  who will hold the Duchess in high regard for her simple \lquote goodness\rquote ? The speech could be read in this way. However, the problem with interpreting it thus is that Bosola\rquote s subsequent actions do not alter sufficiently for it to be considered the point at which he makes a transition. He is still instrumental in her death. That said, remorse is certainly a new element to Bosola\rquote s character towards the play\rquote s close. After the Duchess\rquote s murder, Bosola declares: \lquote What would I do, were this to do again? /I would not change my piece of conscience / For all the wealth of Europe\rquote  (IV. ii. 325\endash 27). Penitence may drive him to take action against the brothers at the play\rquote s end. The language that he uses at this point suggests so: \lquote O penitence, let me truly taste thy cup, / That throws men down, only to raise them up\rquote  (V. ii. 337\endash 38). \par
\par
A fully rounded key player \par
\par
Bosola, it appears, develops into something like a fully rounded character, complete with complexities and contradictions. In Painter\rquote s Palace of Pleasure, on which Webster drew, Bozola (sic) is only a \lquote new Judas and assured manqueller\rquote , literally, man-killer (cited in Neill, p. xix). He is a stock type lacking complexity, not much more than a plot vehicle. In Webster, by contrast, as Neill summarises, he \lquote emerges as a psychologically complex and oddly sympathetic character whom the play\rquote s carefully structured double ending allows to vie with the Duchess herself for the role of tragic protagonist\rquote  (Neill, p. xix). Gibbons takes this line of thinking further: \lquote Webster seems intent on developing Bosola as a complex, unstable figure undergoing real, violent inner change, though he is unable to reform or destroy the system \emdash  in Kafkaesque manner the system devours those who serve it\rquote  (Gibbons, pp. xvii\endash xviii). \par
\par
As a final detail, it is worth noting that the playwright forges for Bosola a certain kinship with the audience. He watches, often from the sidelines, often concealed. Is the audience in alliance with him? At other key moments, he plays a chorus-like role \emdash  interpreting events in accordance with his philosophy of life. This makes him persuasive and of all the characters perhaps closest to being Webster\rquote s mouthpiece. Bosola, then, is both a key player in the play\rquote s events and a quasi-objective voice that stands above or beyond them. As an audience, we are challenged to think carefully about our interpretations of the characters portrayed and how these judgements might have come into being. \par
